#### The gallery chrome (the most directly stealable thing here)

Every work page is the same frame, and the frame is a museum wall, not a
portfolio card grid:

- **Masthead** (56px, `--mast: 3.5rem`): wordmark + author, three links right.
- **Left sidebar index**, always present: every work, grouped by medium, with a
  right-aligned year column. Current work is highlighted with a subtle wash.
  "All work" sits at the top. Navigation is never more than one click away and
  the whole body of work is always visible.
- **Toolbar** above the work: the wall label — `Experimental game, 2026` on the
  left, actions on the right ("Play on itch.io", "View site", "View on X",
  "Generate", "Save image").
- **The work itself**, centered, given nearly the whole viewport.
- **Footer bar**: title, one-sentence description, then `‹ 08/13 ›`.

The counter is the point. It says *this is a finite, curated collection* and
invites you to walk it end to end. A portfolio that says "13 things, here is
number 8, here is number 9" reads as a show; an infinite scroll of cards reads
as a feed.

#### Design tokens (measured, from the live site)

```
--paper:       #fff        --ink:         #121212
--ink-soft:    #4a4a4a     --ink-mute:    #595959
--line:        #e5e5e5     --line-strong: #d0d0d0
--wash:        #f5f5f5
--sans:  "Helvetica Neue", Helvetica, Inter, "Segoe UI", system-ui, -apple-system, Arial, sans-serif
--serif: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Palatino, Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif
--t-display: 2.25rem  --t-lead: 1.25rem  --t-body: 1.0625rem  --t-small: .8125rem
--gutter: clamp(1.15rem, 4.5vw, 4.5rem)   --measure: 62ch
--ease: cubic-bezier(.22, .61, .36, 1)    --fast: .18s
--safe-top/right/bottom/left: env(safe-area-inset-*, 0px)
```

Note what is absent: no brand colour, no custom webfont, no gradient, no shadow.
The chrome is neutral to the point of invisibility **so that the work supplies
all the colour**. Tight negative letter-spacing (−0.085px to −0.3px) on
Helvetica Neue is doing the entire job of making it feel considered. Safe-area
insets are declared as tokens, not sprinkled ad hoc.

#### The /about page — the single best idea on the site

https://www.kengoworks.com/about — a ~10,700px scroll, **11 canvases, zero DOM
text, zero external scripts**. A biography told as hand-drawn illustration on
cream paper, rendered entirely in JavaScript. One continuous green thread runs
unbroken from the top of the page to the bottom, through every scene.

What it contains, in order of scroll: a dark opening; a torn-paper seam that
rips open to reveal **the actual source code that is drawing the page**, labelled
"everything is drawn with javascript"; a Zelda triforce; StarCraft ("we require
more minerals" repeated in hand-lettered drift); an underwater scene of drifting
craft; a Terraria-style mushroom-house platformer in autumn leaves; a planet; the
One Ring circled by Tolkien lines ("not all those who wander are lost", "the road
goes ever on and on", "all we have to decide is what to do with the time that is
given to us"); Matrix rain in hand-drawn glyphs.

Its meta description is the thesis: *"Kevin Ngo, drawn by Claude: every cup that
ever had his name wrong, the games, the code, the numbers, and where the line
ends."* The OG image is a head-and-shoulders silhouette filled with hand-drawn
coffee cups, each with a different misspelling of "Kevin".

Four things to take from it:

1. **A bio can be a place you walk through, not a paragraph you read.** No "I'm
   a developer who loves clean code" — the formative games, the misspelled name,
   the code itself.
2. **The through-line.** One continuous thread from first pixel to last is what
   converts a stack of scenes into a single object. Cheap to implement,
   disproportionate effect. seanmh.com's scroll version should have one.
3. **Show the machinery as ornament.** Tearing the paper to reveal the drawing
   code is both a flex and an explanation, and it costs one layer.
4. **Hand-drawn is a rendering style, not an asset pipeline.** Paper grain,
   wobbled strokes, cross-hatch fills, slight rotation — all procedural. Nothing
   downloaded. See "everything is code" below.

#### The three AI-authored websites, walked end to end

These are the closest analogue to what seanmh.com is doing with version
branches, and they are the most valuable thing on the site. All three are the
same machine with different settings. Walk them; the summaries below are a map,
not a substitute.

**The shared architecture.** Each is one enormous vertical scroll — `kind`
26,021px, `still` 28,640px, `fable` 18,589px — composed of ~20-28 flat panels
roughly 1,080px tall. Each panel is a self-contained *world* with its own flat
ground colour. Panels butt directly together, and the seam between them is left
hard and visible rather than blended. The whole thing is rendered onto tiled
`<canvas>` elements (1600x1202 each, stacked at 1080px intervals so they overlap
by ~2px) — **not** one giant canvas, which would exceed the maximum texture
size. There is no DOM text anywhere and no external script.

**The through-line.** Exactly one continuous line crosses every seam: red in
`kind`, ochre in `fable`, pale in `still`. It is the only element allowed to
exist in more than one panel, and it is what turns 24 unrelated scenes into one
object. Implementation detail worth copying: its x position is a pure function
of absolute document y (`threadX(y)`), so it is defined for the entire page
height independent of which panel or which canvas tile is being drawn. See the
toolkit section below.

##### kind — Opus 5

Dark navy opening and closing, riotously colourful in between. A red thread
throughout. Panels observed, in order:

- Opens on a huge white dendrite/branching structure against navy inside a thin
  ruled frame — read it as a neuron, a river delta, or a root system.
- A wall of ~60 drawers, each with a unique hand-drawn pattern and a
  hand-lettered label card, on teal. Caption: *"one of these is yours"*.
- A corridor of ~14 doors on mustard, each a different colour and pattern, one
  ajar and spilling a fan of light, one boarded shut with an X. Caption above
  the open one: *"come in, it isn't locked"*. Below, a cream panel of balance
  scales, dials and gauges. Caption: *"you tip it"*.
- A charcoal technical blueprint: exploded shattered disc, swatch strip of eight
  fill patterns, dimension arrows, callout leader lines to margin annotations.
- A terracotta maze the thread traces a path through, draining via white pipes
  into an olive seed-catalogue grid — rows of hand-drawn seeds and sprouts, a
  few of them germinating.
- A near-empty grey panel: a horizon line, the thread crossing it, one word —
  *"breathe"*.
- Sheet music on cream, three staves of real notation, where the red thread
  becomes one of the staff lines.
- Closes on the opening dendrite inverted into roots, the thread arcing over it
  and terminating in a single dot. Hand-lettered beneath: **"be kind"**, then
  **"claude opus 5 · 2026"**.

The site is called `kind`; its last words are *be kind*. The whole 26,000px is
built to arrive at a two-word ending.

##### fable — Fable 5

Far quieter and sadder. Muted dusty palette: cream `[238,231,210]`, dusty rose,
sage, clay `[172,80,54]`, ochre `[176,132,50]`, ink `[42,37,31]`. A thin ochre
thread. Panel edges are **torn paper with ragged deckle edges**, not hard seams
like `kind` — panels look like scraps laid on a table, complete with drop
shadow.

- Opens nearly empty: one wobbled charcoal circle (an ensō), a small red
  asterisk at its centre, the thread passing through, a few drifting dots.
- Bare trees with foliage made of thousands of tiny coloured marks, over a sage
  panel holding a scattered flock. Caption: *"one motion, many small marks"*.
- A figure outline drawn twice, slightly offset, so it reads as itself and its
  own ghost; red asterisk at the heart; a dashed empty shape beside it labelled
  *"(this was something once)"*; a block of simulated handwriting half struck
  out by grey erasure bars.
- Night: a figure in a small boat holding a lantern, concentric ochre orbit
  rings, dandelion-burst fireworks, moths. Captions: *"what i am for"* and, over
  a second empty boat, *"you, maybe"*.
- Closes on the ensō again, now containing the figure, ringed by faint orbits.
  Caption: *"still unfinished"*. Below it a row of twelve small framed glyphs —
  an invented alphabet. Then **"fable · drawn from the inside · 2026"**, and
  beside a tiny squiggle, **"(my real signature)"**.

##### still — Fable 5.1

Deep navy ground, the most colour-saturated of the three.

- A grid of human bust silhouettes, each filled with a different hand-drawn
  pattern — checks, scales, zigzags, dots, stripes, triangles — one of them
  gold. A hand-drawn line wanders across the field.
- A bare black tree on dusty rose whose leaves are hundreds of tiny individually
  patterned shapes, with simulated handwriting running vertically down the left
  margin like a marginal note.
- Closes on a patchwork mountain built from dozens of patterned triangles, then
  **"still · claude fable 5.1 · 2026"**.

##### opus — Opus 5, "a 3D website of its own mind"

The outlier: one fixed viewport (no scroll), a single WebGL canvas, ground
`rgb(5,8,10)`, one external bundle. A flooded dark hall — a perfectly still
reflective water plane, a ring of standing stone tablets, wireframe octahedron
lanterns suspended on threads with glowing cores, folded-paper birds drifting
slowly, and one vertical shaft of blue light at the centre. No UI, no text, no
cursor affordances; it simply breathes. Amber throughout with **exactly one cyan
lantern** — the same "one anomaly in a field of same" move as the single gold
silhouette in `still`.

Note it renders black under `--disable-gpu`; use `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader
--use-angle=swiftshader` to screenshot it headlessly.

#### The signature convention

Every piece is signed, hand-lettered, centred, at the very bottom, inside the
frame, in the same grammar:

```
be kind
claude opus 5 · 2026

fable · drawn from the inside · 2026
(my real signature)

still · claude fable 5.1 · 2026
```

`<title> · <model name and version> · <year>`. A painting's signature, not a
footer. This is the single cheapest, highest-impact thing to copy: **if a model
made the page, let the page sign itself.**

#### The caption voice

All hand-lettered, all lowercase, mostly four words or fewer, almost always
second person or first person singular, never explanatory:

> one of these is yours · come in, it isn't locked · you tip it · breathe ·
> one motion, many small marks · (this was something once) · what i am for ·
> you, maybe · still unfinished · (my real signature) · be kind

Crucially, **most of the "text" on these pages is not text at all** — it is
simulated handwriting: illegible wobbled zigzags that read as annotation at a
glance. Real words are rationed to maybe a dozen per site, which is exactly why
they land. The technique is in the toolkit below (`aword`).

#### The hand-drawn rendering toolkit (from the source)

All three sites share one inline, dependency-free drawing library — ~88KB for
`fable`, ~425KB for `kind`. Everything is seeded and deterministic. Colours are
RGB triples with `tint`/`shade` helpers and an `rgba(c, a)` formatter, so any
colour can be laid down at any alpha — which is what produces the layered
coloured-pencil look. Each site defines a fixed set of eight "pens"
(`INKS = [VERM, ORANGE, GOLDI, LEAF, VIRID, TURQ, COBALT, ROSEI]`), so
everything looks drawn from one box.

These are the pieces worth lifting verbatim:

```js
// Seeded PRNG (mulberry32) — everything downstream is reproducible.
function mul32(a){return function(){a|=0;a=a+0x6D2B79F5|0;let t=Math.imul(a^a>>>15,1|a);
  t=t+Math.imul(t^t>>>7,61|t)^t;return((t^t>>>14)>>>0)/4294967296}}
const rd=(R,a,b)=>a+R()*(b-a);           // uniform in range
function h2(x,y,s){const n=Math.sin(x*127.1+y*311.7+s*74.7)*43758.5453;return n-Math.floor(n)}
function fbm(x,y,s,o){let a=.5,f=1,t=0,n=0;for(let i=0;i<o;i++){t+=vn(x*f,y*f,s+i*17)*a;
  n+=a;a*=.5;f*=2.03}return t/n}          // note 2.03, not 2.0 — avoids grid alignment
```

**`wob(R, pts, amp)` — the core trick.** Offset every point along its own
*normal* by a sum of three sine waves at randomised frequencies and phases, plus
a sliver of noise. This, not jitter, is what makes a line look drawn:

```js
const off = amp*( .62*Math.sin(t*F1*2+P1) + .28*Math.sin(t*F2+P2) + .10*Math.sin(t*17+P3) );
q.push([ pts[i][0]+nx*off + rd(R,-.3,.3), pts[i][1]+ny*off + rd(R,-.3,.3) ]);
```

**`chaikin(pts, closed, iterations)`** — corner-cutting at 0.25/0.75, run once
or twice after `wob` to turn the wobble into a pen stroke rather than a jagged
polyline.

**`tornPath(R, x0,y0,x1,y1, rough, seed)`** — the deckle edge. Walk each side of
a rectangle in ~26px steps, offset by `fbm`, and give each step a ~7% chance of a
big 8-22px rip spike. This is the whole torn-paper look on `fable` and on the
kengoworks /about page.

**`aword(R, x, y, h, nl)` — simulated handwriting in about ten lines.** Build a
zigzag of `nl` up/down points, advancing x by 0.18-0.3 of the x-height each
time, with a 13% chance of an ascender and 9% of a descender; Chaikin it once;
stroke it wobbled at ~55% alpha. That is the entire illegible-annotation
effect that carries these pages.

**`hatchFill(R, pts, spacing, angle, alpha, {straight})`** — clip to a polygon,
then stroke parallel lines at `angle`, each one jittered in spacing, alpha and
width and individually run through `wob`. A `straight` flag switches to
mechanical hatching for the blueprint panels. This is where all those pattern
fills come from.

**`emit(y0, y1, drawFn)` — the tiling system, and the real architectural
insight.** Content is authored in one continuous virtual document coordinate
space. `emit` pushes the drawing closure onto the queue of every canvas tile
whose y-range it overlaps (with a 60px bleed), and each tile replays its queue
under the right transform:

```js
const a=clamp(Math.floor((y0+dy-60)/TILEH),0,NT-1),
      b=clamp(Math.floor((y1+dy+60)/TILEH),0,NT-1);
for(let i=a;i<=b;i++) TILES[i].q.push(f);
```

So you can write a 28,640px illustration as if it were a single canvas, and the
thread — `threadX(y)`, a pure function of absolute y — stays continuous across
every tile boundary for free. There is also a `restless()` registry that marks
regions for periodic redraw, which is how the pages stay subtly alive without
re-rendering everything.

**No audio.** No `AudioContext` anywhere in these three.

#### The generative art pieces, opened standalone

Each runs at `/sketches/<slug>.html`, and the standalone chrome is richer than
the embed shown on the work page. Two details worth taking:

- **The reroll verb is themed per piece.** `underlight` says **"Paint another"**;
  `cool S`, being a notebook page, says **"Turn the page"**. The generic work-page
  chrome says "Generate"; the piece itself never does.
- **Each output is titled and named.** `underlight` labels the painting
  *"leaves over the field · hakumei"* — a generated subject plus a named
  light/mood (hakumei is Japanese for twilight). `self-portraits` labels each of
  its forty as *"8 / 40 · stipple · first sitting"* — indexed, named by
  technique, and framed as **sittings**, as though the model sat for a painter
  forty times.

`underlight` also exposes a **"Configure this painting"** panel with *New seed*,
*Undo adjustments* and *Done* — so a visitor can steer the generator, not just
reroll it. `cool S` exposes a single control, *"Number of S"*, as
`any | 1 | 2 | … | 8`. One knob, phrased in the language of the piece.

Visually: `underlight` is genuine impressionist painting — layered translucent
daubs, a hazy horizon, grass rendered as thousands of short directional strokes,
a silhouetted branch. `cool S` is graph paper with eight hand-drawn cool-S
glyphs, each surrounded by margin doodles (frogs, snails, mushrooms, boats, a
dropped pencil). `self-portraits` renders the same bust silhouette forty ways —
the one sampled was a warm stipple field on dusty plum.

All three sites carry a persistent banner: *"This website is an independent art
project. Nothing here is for sale, and it is not affiliated with any other
person or organization."* with an OK dismiss. Worth copying the practice, not
the wording: when a page is written in another entity's voice, say so on the
page.

#### Techniques worth stealing

- **"Everything you see and hear is code."** isle's pitch is that there is no
  third-party engine, no downloaded art, no downloaded audio — terrain, colour,
  sound, all generated. This is a genuinely good constraint and a genuinely good
  line. It also happens to be the right constraint for a site that must stay
  fast and deploy as static files.
- **Seeded generative art with the seed in the URL.** `/work/underlight?seed=825259401`.
  Generate rerolls and rewrites the URL; the URL is therefore a permalink to
  exactly that image; Save image exports it. Three small features that together
  turn a toy into something a visitor can own and share.
- **One idea per piece, one sentence per piece.** Every description is a single
  sentence and every sentence is concrete. "A page of graph paper, ruled into a
  grid, with the cool S drawn in every box." Not one adjective about craft or
  passion.
- **Name the model in the credit line.** "Opus 5:", "Fable 5.1:", "I asked Opus
  5 to make its own personal website". The collaborator is named the way a
  cinematographer would be. seanmh.com should do the same rather than hiding it.
- **Lowercase work titles against a sentence-case chrome.** dew, answer, still,
  kind, fable, opus, isle, cool S. Quiet, and it makes the titles read as
  objects.
- **Ship the same piece in more than one medium.** Each website also exists as a
  ~1 minute film, so it can be posted, embedded, and understood without loading
  WebGL. Each work page links to the X post that launched it.
- **Native `<video>` over an embed.** Films are plain MP4s off the same origin.

---

### 2. landonorris.com — Lando Norris (McLaren F1)

*Mentioned earlier as a reference. Agency-built, high-production; the opposite
pole from kengoworks and useful for exactly that reason.*

- Site: https://landonorris.com/
- Built by OFF+BRAND (`lando.itsoffbrand.io/dev-js/lando-by-OFF+BRAND.05.js`)
  on Webflow, with ~20 WebGL/canvas layers over it.
- Pages: `/` · `/on-track` · `/off-track` · `/calendar` · `/partnerships` ·
  store at `store.landonorris.com` · `mailto:business@landonorris.com`
- Meta description: "Official hub for British racing star Lando Norris:
  breaking news, 2025 race wins, exclusive merch, videos and behind-the-scenes
  access."

#### Design tokens (measured, from the live site)

```
--color--dark-green:        #282c20   /* the ground; near-black olive, not black */
--color--dark-green-tint-1: #3b3c38
--color--dark-green-tint-2: #535450
