Typography: **Brier** (display; bold uppercase with aggressive negative tracking,
down to −3.33px) paired with **Mona Sans Variable** (400/600/700/800, body and
UI). Display face does the shouting, variable sans does the work.

#### Structure

Loader → hero (name, "2025 McLaren Formula 1 Driver", a **Next Race** module with
circuit outline, "BAKU GP", and a laurel-wreath helmet badge reading "MCLAREN F1
SINCE 2019") → "Message from Lando" → a photo run captioned by **place and year**
("Qatar, 2024", "Monaco, 2023", "Battersea, 2024") interleaved with pull quotes
→ a two-door split, **ON TRACK** / **OFF TRACK** → **HELMETS HALL OF FAME**, a
grid of 16 helmets each labelled name + year (Discoball 2025, Dark Glitter 2025,
Porcelain 2024, Japan 2024, GIF 2024, Dark Mode 2024, Las Vegas 2023, Chrome
2023, Beachball 2023, Basketball 2022, Silverstone 2020…) → store CTA → partners
→ socials → footer sign-off: **"ALWAYS BRINGING THE FIGHT."**

#### Techniques worth stealing

- **Near-black with a hue.** `#282c20` (olive) instead of `#000` or `#111`. A
  ground with a hue in it reads as designed; pure black reads as a default. Same
  for the "white" — `#f4f4ed`, a cream, not `#fff`.
- **One loud accent, rationed.** `#d2ff00` appears on roughly one element per
  viewport. Its power comes entirely from scarcity.
- **A design-width scale factor.** `--design-width: 1728`, `--design-unit: 16`,
  `--scale-factor` — the layout is authored at one canvas size and scaled, so
  proportions hold instead of breaking into arbitrary breakpoints.
- **A *very* slow default transition.** `0.75s` with
  `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0.05, 0, 1)` — a hard ease-out that starts fast and
  settles long. Most sites are 200ms and feel cheaper for it.
- **Two doors, not a menu.** The whole site reduces to ON TRACK / OFF TRACK. One
  axis, stated once, big.
- **Caption everything with place and year.** It costs nothing and it turns a
  photo grid into a record.
- **A collection page with an obsessive inventory.** The helmet hall of fame is
  the most-loved thing on the site because it is complete and it is nerdy.
  seanmh.com's equivalent is the version branches — treat them as a catalogue
  with names and dates, not a switcher.
- **A pun in the plumbing.** The loading element is labelled "Load Norris". Put
  one joke where only a person who inspects will find it.

#### What *not* to take

It is a commercial hub: store CTAs, partner logos, an email capture, analytics
(GTM), Webflow CMS, a cookie-consenting stack. seanmh.com has no product to sell
and should not import that furniture. Take the type scale, the ground colour,
the ration of accent, the timing curve, the inventory instinct. Leave the funnel.

---

## Principles carried out of these sites

1. **The chrome is neutral so the work can be loud.** kengoworks proves this in
   the extreme — no brand colour at all. Whatever seanmh.com's frame is, it
   should recede.
2. **Finite and numbered beats infinite and scrolling.** `08/13`. A body of
   work with edges can be walked; a feed can only be abandoned.
3. **One sentence per thing, concrete, no adjectives about craft.** If a
   description could be pasted onto someone else's project, rewrite it.
4. **A through-line.** One element continuous across the whole page or the whole
   site, so it is one object rather than a stack of sections.
5. **Everything is code.** No downloaded art, no downloaded audio, no engine.
   Both a constraint and a pitch, and it keeps the deploy static.
6. **Give the visitor a seed.** Anything generative should reroll, put the seed
   in the URL, and let them save the result.
7. **A hue in the near-black, a cream in the white.** Never `#000`/`#fff` by
   default.
8. **One accent, rationed to about one element per viewport.**
9. **Slow, hard-eased motion.** ~0.75s with a long settle reads as expensive.
10. **Name the collaborator.** If a model wrote it, credit it by name and
    version, on the page, the way a crew credit works.
11. **Hide one joke in the plumbing.** Loader label, console message, view-source
    comment, a 404.
12. **Say so when a page speaks in another voice.** A one-line dismissible
    banner is enough.
13. **Let the page sign itself.** `<title> · <model name and version> · <year>`,
    hand-lettered, centred, at the very bottom inside the frame. A painting's
    signature, not a footer.
14. **Build the whole thing to arrive at four words.** `kind` spends 26,000px
    reaching *be kind*. Decide the last line first, then earn it.
15. **Ration real words; simulate the rest.** A dozen real captions per site,
    lowercase, four words or fewer, second person. Everything else is wobbled
    zigzag that reads as annotation and says nothing. Legible text is expensive
    attention — spend it deliberately.
16. **Hard seams, one crossing element.** Panels of flat colour butted directly
    together look intentional; blending them looks like a gradient nobody
    chose. Let exactly one thing cross every seam.
17. **Wobble along the normal, then Chaikin it.** Three summed sines offset
    perpendicular to the path, smoothed by corner-cutting. This is the
    difference between "hand-drawn" and "jittered".
18. **Author in one coordinate space, render to tiles.** A long illustration
    should be written as if it were one canvas; route each draw call to the
    tiles it overlaps. Continuity across the whole page then costs nothing.
19. **One anomaly in a field of same.** One gold silhouette among forty
    patterned ones; one cyan lantern among a dozen amber. The eye needs
    somewhere to land.
20. **Theme the verb.** A generative piece rerolls with *Paint another* or
    *Turn the page*, never *Generate*. Name every control in the language of
    the thing it controls.
21. **Title the output, not just the piece.** *"leaves over the field ·
    hakumei"*, *"8 / 40 · stipple · first sitting"*. A generated artifact that
    names itself is worth keeping.

## Notes specific to seanmh.com

- The version branches (`a-scroll`, `b-card`, `c-terminal`, `nexus`, `d-3d-game`,
  `e-2d-game`, `f-blueprint`, `g-travel`) are already a body of work in the
  kengoworks sense. They are currently framed as *alternate presentations of one
  site*. Consider also framing them as *a catalogue of thirteen-ish pieces* —
  named, dated, numbered, walkable with prev/next, each with a single-sentence
  description and a wall label. That is the single highest-leverage idea in this
  file.
- The Blueprint systems map at `/systems/` is the closest thing the site has to
  the kengoworks /about page: machinery shown as ornament. It could lean much
  harder into that.
- The scroll version has no through-line. It should get one — and the cheap,
  correct implementation is kengoworks': make its x position a pure function of
  absolute document y, so it is continuous by construction rather than stitched
  per section.
- The hand-drawn toolkit above (seeded PRNG → `wob` → `chaikin` → `hatchFill` →
  `aword` → tiled `emit`) is perhaps 200 lines total and has no dependencies. It
  would drop straight into a new version branch, and it is a far more
  distinctive look than any CSS framework will produce.
- Nothing on seanmh.com currently signs itself. Every version branch that a
  model had a hand in should end with a hand-lettered
  `<name> · <model> · <year>`.
- The `/card` short-URL reasoning in the README (29×29 vs 33×33 QR modules) is
  exactly the kind of obsessive, concrete detail that the helmet hall of fame
  and the isle pitch are made of. More of that, written down where people see
  it, not only in the README.

---

## Fetching notes (operational)

- **kengoworks.com sits behind a Vercel bot checkpoint.** `WebFetch` gets 403 and
  plain `curl` gets 429 with a "Vercel Security Checkpoint" page. `--dump-dom`
  from headless Chrome snapshots before the challenge clears. What works: launch
  Chrome with `--headless=new --remote-debugging-port=<port>`, drive it over CDP,
  override the UA away from `HeadlessChrome` via `Network.setUserAgentOverride`,
  and poll until `document.title` is no longer the checkpoint. Node 22 has a
  global `WebSocket`, so no dependency is needed.
- **Most of these pages are canvas, so `innerText` is empty.** `/about`,
  `/kind`, `/fable` and `/still` carry no DOM text at all. Screenshot and look;
  do not conclude a page is broken because it has no text.
- **Screenshot with a GPU if you care about the 3D pieces.** `/opus` is a single
  WebGL canvas and renders black under `--disable-gpu`.
